  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 956 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nONGOING FLOODING\n\nThe main low pressure responsible for all of the recent \nrainfall is currently located over Nevada and California. This \nhas kept us in southwest flow aloft. Most of the deep tropical \nmoisture has pushed east of the area with the exception of \nsouth central Colorado. This is where isolated to scattered \nshowers and storms will be possible mainly this afternoon. These\nshowers will be capable of producing strong winds, hail and \nheavy rainfall. The main concern is more rainfall for this \nregion, which is already experience flooding. Given the ground \nis very saturated it might not take much more rainfall to cause \nadditional flooding. Tonight and tomorrow the low pressure \nbegins to approach from southern California. As that happens it \nwill draw moisture back into the area. Therefore we will see an \nuptick in showers and storms starting tonight and continuing \ntomorrow. The coverage during this time frame expands to \nlocations mainly along and south of I-70. This means additional \nrainfall for places that have already experienced excessive \nrainfall. The rainfall amounts through Sunday are expected to \nrange from 0.5-1.0 inches along the highway 160 corridor with \nslightly higher amounts in the mountains and lower amounts \noutside of southwest Colorado. Training storms could result in \n1-3 inches, which could result in more flooding and a\ncontinuation of ongoing flooding. The low pressure lifts \noverhead on Sunday so expect more showers across the area. \nHowever these showers should be relatively light compared to the\npast few days. Conditions dry out on Monday as the low pressure\nlifts out over the Plains.\n\nAs we move towards next week, models are also in fairly good \nagreement that dry zonal flow will begin to propagate the region\nby Monday. Because of this, we will be seeing cooler than \nnormal temperatures, with the potential for overnight lows to \ndrop below freezing for a couple hours at a time. Keep in mind, \nthis is still relatively far out, so confidence remains low on \nthis matter.",
